{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"01b7da3f9559623d","type":"text","text":"## Soul-Making Theodicy\nGod didn't create humanity as a finished, perfect product. The earth is designed to build virtues. We can't develop courage without danger, nor compassion without witnessing pain, nor perseverance without obstacles.","x":1709,"y":-41,"width":433,"height":248,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"a30f5bf6122e6414","type":"text","text":"This is good for explaining moral evil, but what about other types?","x":1796,"y":-572,"width":240,"height":121,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"c67fb4ba2d5c0261","type":"text","text":"## Natural Law Theodicy\nFor free will and soul-making to work, humans need to live in a predictable universe with consistent physical laws. If God constantly intervened with miracles to stop a rock from hitting your head, science, learning, and meaningful choices would be impossible.","x":1809,"y":283,"width":455,"height":273,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"b0567743d8c2c195","type":"text","text":"Is this ad-hoc? And is the free will of demons enough to justify the suffering of trillions of animals? Or the sin of a very distant human ancestor couple?","x":2012,"y":-318,"width":401,"height":150,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"c12aecda37dfafba","type":"text","text":"This is good for a lot of everyday hardship, however it doesn't explain pointless suffering like an infant who dies at two months old or things like animal suffering. Also, conceivably it might be possible that God could create us as a finished perfect product. Why does courage need to be developed through experiencing danger instead of just being programmed in?","x":2253,"y":-72,"width":466,"height":279,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"2cc84dd4ee3348e9","type":"text","text":"This is good at explaining natural disasters and accidents, however it limits God's creativity. Couldn't pain receptors turn off after a certain threshold? Why couldn't the physical laws be designed in a way that is more favorable to us that won't have as much or any natural evil?","x":2365,"y":317,"width":536,"height":204,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"f822dc42af1f32b8","type":"text","text":"This severely limits God's omnipotence. Couldn't God have designed a functional ecosystem of plant-eating herbivores?","x":2324,"y":655,"width":378,"height":126,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"5e56d0ed385ff771","type":"text","text":"Modern science (e.g. The Cambridge Declaration on Consciousness) rejects this view. Furthermore, an animal's inability to rationalize why it is in pain arguably makes the terror worse, not better.","x":1991,"y":892,"width":374,"height":171,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"d7db451c72e7245b","type":"text","text":"## Animal Heaven\nGod is perfectly just, so animals will be resurrected, given upgraded cognition, or somehow compensated in an eternal afterlife.","x":1298,"y":1174,"width":411,"height":194,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"d5885fb182edf3f1","type":"text","text":"## The Fortunate Fall\nThe greatest possible good in the universe is the Incarnation and Atonement. This cannot happen unless there is sin and suffering to atone for. Therefore a world with sin, suffering, and the cross is vastly better than a peaceful world where God never demonstrates that level of radical love.","x":1195,"y":1429,"width":524,"height":248,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"72a3507d5188bc70","type":"text","text":"Compensation does not equal justification. It's like giving a child a million dollars after torturing them, and God could have skipped the torture in the first place.","x":1799,"y":1189,"width":414,"height":165,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"ea9190bc91d14e48","type":"text","text":"## Neo-Cartesian\nAnimals only have physical pain (nociception), but lack the prefrontal cortex for higher-order \"awareness\" that they are suffering.","x":1514,"y":867,"width":360,"height":221,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"d18b82e33b843cca","type":"text","text":"## Only Way Possible\nDarwinian evolution is the only logically possible way to create a universe that \"makes itself\". Suffering is a necessary tax.","x":1766,"y":617,"width":480,"height":203,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"f951a61a73153d2f","type":"text","text":"## Nuclear Response\nSkeptical Theism is a \"nuclear\" defense that destroys human logic. If we can't trust our moral intuitions about extreme evil, we face **Moral Paralysis** (why stop a murder if God is using it for a hidden good?) and **Epistemic Contagion** (If God has hidden reasons for allowing evil, He could have hidden reasons for deceiving us about reality or scripture).","x":-178,"y":729,"width":580,"height":280,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"e5ca1cde8bc7665e","type":"text","text":"## Ultimate Takeaway\nIf you look at the universe without a prior basis, the evidence points to blind, pitiless indifference. The theist's primary viable tactic is to provide independent evidence for God, and then use Skeptical Theism to accept many of the evils that theodicies don't account for as a mystery.","x":458,"y":729,"width":520,"height":240,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"d54282caeb3b8936","type":"text","text":"If we are totally ignorant of God's hidden reasons, then hidden goods and hidden evils are equally probable cancelling each other out. Thus, we should default to what we can see.","x":-733,"y":760,"width":392,"height":219,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"e9be43190929788c","type":"text","text":"That math assumes a neutral observer. However given independent, positive evidence for God's existence, we should expect hidden goods over hidden evils. Skeptical Theism works as a shield given the independent evidence for God is strong enough.","x":-810,"y":1091,"width":509,"height":180,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"45b4dac78e1b0904","type":"text","text":"If the debate rests on the the prior probability of God against the evidence of evil, we need to calculate the actual weight of the evil.\n\nWile Animal Suffering. 99.9% of all sentient life is r-selected (e.g. frogs, insects, fish). To maintain a stable population, the mother will lay 10,000 eggs, and 9,998 of those offspring must die shortly after birth. From starving, freezing, or being eaten alive by parasites.\n\nThis has been happening for hundreds of millions of years before humans existed. It builds no human character. This ratio of uncompensated agony to non-suffering should crush the theist's prior probability for God","x":-182,"y":1119,"width":640,"height":359,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"d431ed1bfa76086e","type":"text","text":"How strong is the prior probability for God?","x":671,"y":1119,"width":238,"height":99,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"5a97ffb5777c790d","type":"text","text":"Reject Divine Simplicity.","x":-767,"y":2965,"width":267,"height":52},
		{"id":"dc79710f321c204a","type":"text","text":"## The Euthyphro Dilemma\n\n1. Is something good because God commands it? If so morality is arbitrary\n2. Does God command something because it is good? If so morality is independent of God\n\nEither way, this is bad for the theist. God can't be goodness itself if you reject DDS.","x":-754,"y":3126,"width":427,"height":300,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"26eb000b9b8cfffd","type":"text","text":"Accept MDCT.","x":-617,"y":3503,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"61069ec9dc8ec3a3","type":"text","text":"## Modified Divine Command Theory\n\nOur moral duties come from God's commands, but they aren't arbitrary because they are rooted in God's perfectly good, unchangeable nature. He is the measuring stick for the universe.","x":-596,"y":3673,"width":527,"height":209,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"0a3366aa45010f9c","type":"text","text":"Reject MDCT.","x":-307,"y":3503,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"6706ec0959d45e3e","type":"text","text":"It seems like God is now just a maximal superhero instead of the absolute ground of all reality.","x":-277,"y":2410,"width":294,"height":142,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"d5355a03e26ff211","type":"text","text":"If God has a nature (but isn't identical to it) and has properties (omniscience, goodness), then properties are abstract objects that exist distinct from God. There would then be an infinite realm of uncreated abstract objects God didn't create.","x":-247,"y":2918,"width":395,"height":198,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"00849469e403f0f1","type":"text","text":"Everything composed of parts requires a \"composer\" to put those parts together, because the parts are logically prior to the whole. What unites God's parts? There must be a higher metaphysical principle, and so God is not the first cause.","x":-246,"y":2610,"width":406,"height":208,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"7cca75cade555fba","type":"text","text":"Accept Nominalism.","x":257,"y":3155,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"f2d05929041f92b2","type":"text","text":"This is a category mistake, a mind is not a machine. Intellect and will are simply capacities of one substance, so God doesn't need a composer.\n\nOr perhaps God's properties are logically mutually entailing, like the fact that a triangle has three angles and three sides.","x":272,"y":2510,"width":469,"height":232,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"a1a5aa7217c892c2","type":"text","text":"## Nominalism\n\nAbstract objects (numbers, properties, logic) don't exist independently in reality. They are either useful human fictions or concepts existing inside a mind.","x":355,"y":2855,"width":371,"height":221,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"9c93ad7684ed2bd2","type":"text","text":"Reject Nominalism.","x":578,"y":3155,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"4ae6386c1d46b78f","type":"text","text":"Accept Divine Simplicity.","x":-1140,"y":2965,"width":264,"height":52},
		{"id":"58aa5e9066f886ca","type":"text","text":"If God is Pure Act, how can the universe be contingent? Along the same line of reasoning, how can we have free will if our creation was contingent, because God's knowledge about our actions isn't able to change or update under DDS.","x":-1287,"y":3116,"width":411,"height":216,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"597694fded2e26a4","type":"text","text":"Our human brains are limited, so this is simply a mystery.","x":-1303,"y":3426,"width":279,"height":77,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"e04d2b91287f5030","type":"text","text":"Why is God's nature loving rather than cruel? There is no higher standard or reason, it just is. Ultimately it seems like the foundation of all morality is just a brute fact.","x":-1133,"y":3636,"width":379,"height":171,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"271e9d67d0fa3d0b","type":"text","text":"God \"super comprehends\" what every possible creaturely essence says ","x":-1839,"y":3033,"width":338,"height":93,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"3895411c33eb2308","type":"text","text":"## Molinism\n\nMolinism divides God's mind into three logical moments:\n\n1. Natural Knowledge (necessary truths and possibilities)\n2. Middle Knowledge (counterfactuals - what free creatures would do)\n3. Free Knowledge (God created and world, and knows what will happen)","x":-1896,"y":2049,"width":560,"height":303,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"c73cb0a851b7e476","type":"text","text":"Accept Molinism.","x":-1920,"y":2450,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"bf738b75ae0e90c1","type":"text","text":"Reject Molinism.","x":-1560,"y":2450,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"5639dd5e09687a39","type":"text","text":"When someone dies not in a state of grace and goes to hell, what if that person had lived a bit longer and achieved a state of grace? Or vice versa, like if St. Augustine had died at 20? Without middle knowledge of what we would do how can God fairly weight our actions? Although even still it might be problematic because how do you weigh actions given infinite scenarios - is there a threshold for universes where you choose God where you can go to heaven\n\nAnd Molinism should be incompatible with Divine Simplicity, because God's middle knowledge is passive and dependent on our free will","x":-1729,"y":2552,"width":589,"height":343,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"1da55b9180746006","type":"text","text":"## Doctrine of Divine Simplicity\n\nGod is divinely simple and is not composed of parts. He is His properties, and is the absolute, unquestionable ground of all reality and Being Itself (Ipsum Esse Subsistens).\n","x":-1052,"y":2670,"width":462,"height":211,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"669b8624dcdb2b8a","type":"text","text":"## Theodicies\nEvil can be justified!","x":934,"y":364,"width":236,"height":125,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"6570dc75a4a153d2","type":"text","text":"## Free Will Theodicy\nFree will is an unsurpassably great good. God would want a world with genuinely free creatures who can choose to love God and each other. However it is logically impossible to do so and not allow the possibility of evil, which humans actualized.","x":1230,"y":-640,"width":483,"height":249,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"f03b1d443830e350","type":"text","text":"## The Fallen World / Demonic Corruption\nGod didn't invent predation and parasites, either the sin of Adam and Eve or demons are the reason for a broken world.","x":1507,"y":-333,"width":374,"height":215,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"764bf826be5ed57a","type":"text","text":"Departs from classical omnipotence","x":112,"y":-266,"width":235,"height":78,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"972bb42b81798300","type":"text","text":"# The Problem of Evil\n\n1. God would not allow unjustified evil to exist\n2. Unjustified evil does exist\n3. God does not exist\n\nPremise 2 is highly probable due to a \"noseeum\" inference. Take a fawn which burns to death in a forest fire. There doesn't seem to be any greater good to justify that, so it probably isn't there. Therefore Premise 3 is probably true.","x":231,"y":236,"width":520,"height":340,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"8c3bd04afc216658","type":"text","text":"## Open Theism\nGod knows all possibilities, but doesn't know the future. He takes risks and sometimes they go wrong.","x":560,"y":-54,"width":453,"height":175,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"ef9cc93e5b7e77f9","type":"text","text":"Departs from classical omniscience","x":641,"y":-272,"width":270,"height":84,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"02759cc086605a80","type":"text","text":"## Skeptical Theism\nThe \"noseeum\" inference is flawed. The cognitive gap between an infinite God and a human is by definition infinite. It would be like a baby trying to understand a vaccine.\n\nWe should only expect to see a reason if it's the kind of thing we could see. We shouldn't expect to comprehend God's reasons, therefore our failure to see a reason is not evidence that no reason exists.","x":-537,"y":236,"width":520,"height":320,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"98bef1c219b24f98","type":"text","text":"## Process Theology\nGod is not all powerful. He simply does His best to persuade the universe toward goodness.","x":40,"y":-72,"width":380,"height":202,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"0c648cd432311e1d","type":"text","text":"A brute fact implies contingency about it. However God's nature is not contingent, it couldn't be different from what it is.\n\nSince loving, just, and fair are \"great making properties\", by the Ontological argument God is those things.","x":-1790,"y":3585,"width":503,"height":222,"color":"4"}
	],
